# Reason: A game that was just added has loose .rpy files with no valid .rpyc. Script.load_file parses them one after the other on one core: 76 percent of a cold load of a large script after lazy compilation. Forked workers now compile the files first (renpy/parallelcompile.py), in waves that end at every file with a python early block or an early config value (renpy/scriptscan.py reads the text for them), and write the same .rpyc files into the cache. Script.load_script then loads every file in the same order as before and finds a valid .rpyc for each, so node order, init priorities, the namemap, the duplicate label check and the early blocks are those of a serial load. One thing in a compiled file depends on the files before it: translate identifiers are unique across the script (two files that both start with the same license text get different identifiers in a serial load). Each worker reports the identifiers of a file, and a file that has one that an earlier file has loses its compiled file, so the load compiles it again. At most 4 workers and two cores stay free; they are reaped when the load ends or stops early. A launch with nothing to compile only reads and hashes the sources. Where fork is missing (Windows), on macOS, with fewer than two workers or for little source the load stays serial. RENPY_PARALLEL_COMPILE=N sets the number of workers (0 turns it off).

